News - New slides of “hyperbolic metamaterials” will improve the analytical power of light microscopes
New slides of “hyperbolic metamaterials” will improve the analytical power of light microscopes
Since the resolution limit of most conventional optical microscopes is only 200 nanometers, scientists at the University of California, San Diego, are considering a new type of glass slide coated with “hyperbolic metamaterials” to improve its analytical power even further. The material consists of nanoscale layers of silica glass and alternating layers of silver, which can improve the resolution of microscope slides up to 40 nanometers.
When light passes through the coating, its wavelength is shortened and the light is scattered to create a pattern of spots. A sample material on a slide allows a spotted pattern of short-wavelength light to shine from multiple angles to produce a series of low-resolution images.
